  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Temperatures peak today and Friday as flow \nturns more southwesterly and low-level thicknesses peak, with \nhighs in the low to mid 90s. Dew points also peak on Friday, \nresulting in maximum heat index values returning to around 100F.\nAt this time the odds of issuing a Heat Advisory continue to be\nless than 10%, but trends will continue to be monitored.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mid- level subsidence continues aloft, keeping\nlow end shower chances (15%) confined to areas where \nsea/sound/river breezes converge today. Brought down PotThunder\nto keep thunder mentions out of the forecast today, as a notable\ndry layer above 700mb will make it difficult for updrafts to \ngrow tall enough for lightning.\n\nFriday the high continues to shift offshore and a front sweeps \nsouth through the Mid-Atlantic region. Ahead of this front, \ncontinuous warm, moist advection in southerly flow will help \nbuild PWATs to near 2\". This will bring scattered sea breeze\nshowers/tstorms back into the forecast. Coverage may be limited\nas models are suggesting mid level subsidence and a dry layer \nlingering into Friday. Guidance continues to lock in on a\nsolution where the front largely bisecting eastern NC by \nFriday, although a small minority still shows it stalling \nfarther north along the VA border. Wherever the boundary ends up\nsetting up will be the focus for the highest risk of training \nshowers and thunderstorms contributing to a localized flash \nflooding threat. Forecast 0-6km shear looks to be around 15-25\nknots along the front, giving the northern tier of counties (hwy\n264 and areas north, the best chance of organized convection and\nhigher rainfall totals.\n\nThe front lifts back north Saturday into Sunday as a shortwave\nmoves through NC. This shortwave will bring slightly better \nchances for showers and thunderstorms this weekend. Like Friday,\nflooding concerns remain the primary risk with PWATs still \naround 2\" but effective shear slightly higher at 15-20 kt. \nAvailable instability remains a question mark for that day given\npotential convective evolution on Friday and the risk for \nlingering convective debris overnight and/or low stratus that \nmay develop on the cooler side of the frontal boundary. The NBM \nprobabilistic envelope has shifted slightly cooler for Saturday \nbut still remains high compared to both deterministic and MOS \nguidance, suggesting a cooler and therefore lower instability \nsolution will prevail. If more instability can develop, the \nmodest uptick in shear may provide a slightly more favorable \nenvironment for an isolated stronger storm or two. One deviation\nfrom previous model run iterations of note is the potential for\nthe shortwave to move through to our west. This would decrease\noverall forcing in eastern NC and confine storms to areas\ngenerally west of hwy 17. This trend has been reflected in\nslightly lower PoPs for Saturday afternoon/evening (down from \n80% to 70% inland), and a PoP free forecast for much of the \ncoastline.\n\nThere is higher confidence on a low- CAPE outcome Sunday (less\nthan 2000 J/kg). While heavy showers and some thunderstorms are\npossible with the shortwave, the severe threat appears to be \nmore muted.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A more typical summertime pattern will settle in\nagain early next week as a large high sits over the southeast.\nBased on current model outputs, we have enough moisture and \ninstability present for some afternoon/evening sea breeze \nconvection early next week, but there are no larger scale\nsynoptic features of note that would promote any additional\nforcing.",
